



{"id":"a7bc029c-4653-47c5-a74e-e039cc36c5de","object":"chat.completion","created":1786447551,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_057\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":17,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":3,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_10\",\"depart_slot\":6,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":9,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_01\",\"depart_slot\":5,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_08\",\"depart_slot\":13,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV repair optimization. The task requires returning exactly one JSON object with the specified schema. The baseline provides a candidate solution. We must produce a repair that covers every mission exactly once, respecting fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and intervention overrides. We need to optimize lexicographically: urgent holds (none?), total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe are given the baseline decisions, but we are told the scenario is \"binding\" and we need to produce a repair. Possibly the baseline is the original plan, but there are interventions (closures, overrides, etc.) that make it infeasible? The task says \"Produce one complete integrated UAV repair for the perturbed scenario.\" So we need to adjust the decisions to satisfy all constraints under the interventions.\n\nFirst, understand the instance. There are three UAVs: UAV_01, UAV_02, UAV_03. The assignment order contract is \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\", meaning each mission is assigned to specific UAV and the sequence within that UAV is fixed (sequence_index). So we cannot reassign missions to different UAVs, and the order per UAV is given. So we must respect the sequence indices: for each UAV, missions must be scheduled in increasing sequence_index order. For UAV_01: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_04 (seq 1). For UAV_02: MISSION_01 (seq 0). For UAV_03: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1), MISSION_06 (seq 2).\n\nDeadhead contract: \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\". However, all missions have \"incoming_deadhead\": null. So no deadhead.\n\nMissions have origin and destination vertiports, release and due slots, path options (with energy, travel slots, wind levels, corridor usage, etc.). We must choose a path, departure slot, and charge_blocks for each mission. Charge_blocks can be 0-3. Note: \"The charge_blocks attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround.\" Turnaround slots is 1 (global). So each mission after completing its travel and reserve check (immediately after incoming-deadhead+service) will then have a turnaround of 1 slot, then post-service charge for the number of blocks. The charge energy per block depends on UAV: charge_kwh_per_block. UAV_01: 1.2 kWh/block, UAV_02: 1.2 kWh/block, UAV_03: 0.95 kWh/block.\n\nWe have interventions:\n- Closed corridor slots: COR_08 is closed from slot 5 to 28 (so essentially all slots from 5 onwards? Horizon slots 29, slots 0-28). So any path using COR_08 during those slots is invalid. Which path options use COR_08? MISSION_03 has PATH_05 (COR_08), and MISSION_03 release slot 5, due 22. Since COR_08 is closed from slot 5 onwards, PATH_05 is unusable unless the travel occurs before slot 5. But release is 5, travel slots is 2, so departure slot must be >=5? Release slot is the earliest departure slot. So if we depart at slot 5, the travel occupies slots 5 and 6 (since travel slots=2). Both slots COR_08 is closed. So PATH_05 is infeasible. MISSION_03 alternative paths: PATH_07 uses COR_09, PATH_10 uses COR_11. So we must choose one of those. Baseline chose PATH_05, which is now infeasible.\n- Pad capacity overrides: VP_05 pad capacity=1 at slot 4. That means at slot 4, only one aircraft can be at VP_05 (arriving/departing?). Pad capacity applies per slot for the number of simultaneous pad operations (takeoff/landing)? Typically pad capacity limits the number of aircraft that can be at the vertiport at a given time (arrival+departure). The override reduces capacity to 1 at slot 4. We need to check if that causes conflicts.\n- Initial SOC overrides: UAV_01 initial SOC changed to 1.690825 kWh (from the baseline instance's own initial SOC? Baseline fleet data: UAV_01 initial_soc_kwh 2.740825, so reduced). So UAV_01 starts with less energy. That may affect its missions.\n- No charger capacity overrides, corridor capacity overrides, mission window overrides.\n\nWe must check the baseline decisions to see if they become infeasible under these interventions and generate a feasible repair.\n\nBaseline decisions:\nUAV_01:\n- MISSION_05: depart_slot 2, path_id PATH_01 (COR_05), charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_04: depart_slot 6, path_id PATH_02 (COR_07), charge_blocks 0\n\nUAV_02:\n- MISSION_01: depart_slot 17, path_id PATH_09 (COR_06), charge_blocks 0\n\nUAV_03:\n- MISSION_02: depart_slot 2, path_id PATH_06 (COR_02), charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_03: depart_slot 5, path_id PATH_05 (COR_08), charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_06: depart_slot 13, path_id PATH_08 (COR_04), charge_blocks 0\n\nNow check feasibility with interventions:\n\nFor UAV_03 MISSION_03 with PATH_05: COR_08 closed from slot 5 onwards. Depart slot 5, travel slots 2, so corridor used slots 5-6? The edge_segment shows one segment with duration 2, offset 0. That means the corridor is used for slots departure+offset to departure+offset+duration-1? Usually, a path's travel slots specify the total travel time slots, and edge_segments give the sequence of corridor usage with their own durations and offset slots from departure. For PATH_05: edge_segments: [{\"corridor_id\":\"COR_08\",\"duration_slots\":2,\"offset_slot\":0,\"wind_level\":1}]. So this segment uses COR_08 from depart_slot + 0 to depart_slot+0+2-1 = depart_slot and depart_slot+1. So if depart_slot=5, it uses slots 5 and 6. Both closed. So infeasible. So we need to change that.\n\nFor UAV_01 MISSION_05: depart_slot 2, PATH_01 uses COR_05, which is not closed. Initial SOC is 1.690825. Check energy: PATH_01 energy_kwh=0.144495. Reserve check after incoming deadhead+service flight. Incoming deadhead is null, so energy consumption is just service flight energy (0.144495). Then we must check that after that deduction, remaining SOC >= reserve (1.5). Starting SOC: 1.690825. After deducting 0.144495, remaining = 1.54633, which is > 1.5 reserve. So fine. Then turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge (if any). charge_blocks=0, so no charge. Then MISSION_04: depart_slot 6. Need to ensure UAV_01 has enough energy after any charging. UAV_01 after MISSION_05 at VP_05 (destination of PATH_01) will be at VP_05. MISSION_04 starts at VP_05 (origin). So no deadhead. But the sequence order: MISSION_05 is seq 0, MISSION_04 is seq 1. So after MISSION_05 ends (arrival at VP_05), the UAV needs to undergo turnaround and then be ready for MISSION_04. MISSION_05 depart_slot=2, travel slots=2, so arrival slot = 2+2-1? Actually travel slots is the number of slots the flight takes. Usually departure slot is the slot the flight starts, and it is in the air for travel_slots, arriving at the destination after that many slots, i.e., arrival at slot depart+travel_slots? But careful: If travel_slots=2, and depart slot=2, then the flight occupies slots 2 and 3? Typically, if a flight takes 2 slots, it departs at slot 2, flies during slot 2 and 3? Or maybe the flight duration is 2 slots meaning it leaves at start of slot 2 and arrives at end of slot 2? But in these tasks, travel_slots is the number of planning slots the flight occupies. It might be that departure slot is the slot in which the flight starts, and it is \"in the air\" for that many slots, arriving at the end of that period. So arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? Or maybe arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots? Need to check convention. The reserve check is \"immediately after incoming deadhead plus service energy are deducted\". So after flight, the UAV's SOC is reduced by energy, then reserve check. Then turnaround of 1 slot occurs. Then post-service charge. So the UAV becomes available for next mission after turnaround and charge. So if a mission departs at slot D, travel T, arrival at slot A = D + T - 1? Or A = D + T? Let's check baseline example: MISSION_05 depart 2, travel 2, then MISSION_04 depart 6. The gap from departure 2 to next departure 6 is 4 slots. If arrival is at slot 2+2=4, then turnaround of 1 slot (slot 5?), then next departure at slot 6. That fits: arrival slot 4, turnaround slot 5, next mission departs at slot 6. So arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots (since travel 2 slots, depart 2, arrival at the end of slot 3? Actually if flight takes 2 slots and departs at start of slot 2, it would be in the air for slot 2 and slot 3, arriving at the end of slot 3, so available for turnaround at slot 4). That gives a gap: depart 2, travel 2 -> arrival at end of slot 3, turnaround at slot 4, next depart 6? That would leave slot 5 idle. That's possible, but baseline depart 6 is after turnaround at slot 4 and then slot 5 idle. The baseline might have chosen later slot to meet release? MISSION_04 release slot is 6, so it cannot depart before 6. So depart 6 is fine. So maybe arrival time is at the end of the last travel slot, i.e., after travel slots elapse. So if travel_slots = T, departure at D, then the flight occupies D, D+1, ..., D+T-1, and arrives just before D+T. Turnaround of 1 slot occupies slot D+T (the arrival slot is the next slot after travel? Actually turnaround time after arrival: after the flight, the UAV is on the ground and needs 1 slot turnaround before it can depart again. So if arrival at the end of D+T-1, then the turnaround happens in slot D+T, and the UAV can depart at D+T+1 at the earliest. In baseline, MISSION_05 travel 2, depart 2: following slots: flight slots 2,3. Arrival at end of slot 3. Turnaround in slot 4. Earliest next departure would be slot 5. But release of MISSION_04 is 6, so departing at 6 is feasible. So that interpretation is consistent.\n\nAlternatively, they might define arrival at the slot when travel ends, and that slot is also the turnaround start? It's ambiguous but we can use the baseline as a reference: For MISSION_05 depart 2, travel 2, next mission depart 6. Turnaround is 1 slot. So if flight occupies 2 slots, the UAV is busy during slots 2 and 3 (flight), then turnaround in slot 4, then idle in slot 5, then next flight departs at 6. That would mean arrival occurs at the end of slot 3? That fits: turnaround in slot 4, then UAV ready at start of slot 5? Actually if turnaround occurs in slot 4, after that the UAV is ready for dispatch at the start of slot 5. So earliest depart could be 5. So with release 6, it departs at 6. So okay.\n\nWe need to respect pad capacity. Pad capacity default 2. Override: VP_05 slot 4 capacity=1. So at slot 4, only one UAV can be at VP_05 (either arriving, on ground, or departing). The pad capacity is per slot for number of UAVs present. Need to check if any conflict. In baseline: At slot 4, what UAVs are at VP_05? UAV_01 arrives at end of slot 3 from MISSION_05, then turnaround in slot 4, so it is on the ground at VP_05 during slot 4 (turnaround). UAV_02: start at VP_05, but its first mission is MISSION_01 depart 17, so it's idle at its start vertiport? Start vertiport for UAV_02 is VP_05. So UAV_02 is at VP_05 from the start? Available slot 2 for UAV_02 means it is available from slot 2? Actually fleet: \"available_slot\": 2 for each UAV, meaning the UAV is available to start missions from that slot onwards. So UAV_02 is available at VP_05 from slot 2. It does not depart until slot 17. So it remains at VP_05 from slot 2 to 16, occupying a pad? Typically, an idle UAV at a vertiport does not consume pad capacity? Or does it? Pad capacity typically limits the number of simultaneous aircraft on the ground or arriving/departing. Some models count idle aircraft as occupying pad capacity. We need to check. The resources include pad_capacity_by_slot_default. Usually, pad capacity is for the number of aircraft that can be at the vertiport at any given time, including parked. If so, then UAV_02 idle at VP_05 would occupy a pad slot continuously from its start until it departs. That would mean at slot 4, if pad capacity is reduced to 1, and UAV_02 is there and UAV_01 arrives and does turnaround at VP_05, that might violate capacity (2 at slot 4). But we need to see if the intervention is designed to cause a conflict that requires a repair. The baseline has UAV_01 arriving and doing turnaround at slot 4, while UAV_02 is idle there. That could be the conflict. Since capacity override reduces to 1 at slot 4, and both would be present, that's a violation. So we must adjust schedules to avoid this.\n\nWe could potentially hold one of the missions or adjust departure times. Since assignment is fixed and order fixed, we might adjust MISSION_05 departure time to avoid overlap, or move MISSION_04, or maybe change the path of MISSION_05 to a different path that does not end at VP_05? But MISSION_05 destination is VP_05 (origin VP_04, destination VP_05). Paths both go to VP_05. So it must go to VP_05. So arrival at VP_05 is inevitable. Could we delay MISSION_05 to depart later so that it doesn't arrive during slot 4? Or earlier? If depart earlier, say slot 0 or 1? Release slot is 2, so earliest departure is 2. So it can't be earlier. Could depart later, e.g., slot 3? Then arrival would be at end of slot 4 (travel 2, depart 3 -> flights 3,4, arrival end of 4, turnaround 5). So at slot 4, during flight, it's not on the ground, so pad capacity may not apply to aircraft in flight. But then UAV_02 idle would still be at VP_05 at slot 4. If idle uses pad capacity, that still leaves one pad occupied. At slot 4, UAV_01 is in the air, so only one on ground (UAV_02). That would be okay with capacity 1. So if we delay MISSION_05 departure to slot 3, then MISSION_05 will be in the air during slot 4, so no pad conflict at VP_05 slot 4. However, MISSION_05 due slot is 8. Depart slot 3 + travel 2 = arrival end of slot 4, then turnaround slot 5, then it's ready from slot 6. That's before due 8, so fine. Then MISSION_04 release 6, could depart at 6. That might work. But we must check if MISSION_05 departure slot 3 is allowed: release slot 2, so ok. Also check energy for MISSION_05: initial SOC 1.690825, energy for PATH_01 is 0.144495, remaining after flight 1.54633 > 1.5 reserve. So fine. Then MISSION_04 departing at 6 from VP_05: origin VP_05, destination VP_02, path PATH_02 energy 0.09633. After MISSION_05, UAV_01 will have some SOC. But we need to account for any charging after MISSION_05. Charge_blocks=0, so no charge after MISSION_05. The UAV_01's SOC after MISSION_05 turnaround is at the start of slot 6? Actually after turnaround at slot 5, the UAV is ready to start next mission at the beginning of slot 6. So SOC at departure of MISSION_04 is the SOC after MISSION_05 flight and no charge. That is 1.54633. Then MISSION_04 uses PATH_02 energy 0.09633, leaving 1.45, which is less than reserve 1.5. So reserve check after MISSION_04 service flight would fail! So that's a problem. Let's compute exactly: initial SOC 1.690825, minus 0.144495 = 1.54633. Then after MISSION_04 deduction 0.09633, remaining = 1.45 exact? 1.54633 - 0.09633 = 1.45000. Reserve is 1.5, so it's below. So that fails. So MISSION_04 cannot be flown without charging. So we might need to charge after MISSION_05. If we add some charge_blocks after MISSION_05, we could boost SOC before MISSION_04. Also note UAV_01 battery capacity is 16 kWh, so plenty. Charge per block 1.2 kWh. After MISSION_05, SOC = 1.54633 (at arrival). Then turnaround 1 slot, then we can charge for some blocks. Post-service charge occurs at the destination after the turnaround. So after MISSION_05, we can schedule charge_blocks to recharge. But charge_blocks must be non-negative integer up to 3. If we set charge_blocks=1, SOC after charge = min(initial + charge_blocks*1.2, battery_capacity) but after MISSION_05, SOC might be less than capacity. So 1.54633 + 1.2 = 2.74633, below 16. Then MISSION_04 energy 0.09633, remaining 2.65, above reserve. So that works. However, adding charge blocks increases waiting time and may affect pad/charger capacity. So we need to check charger capacity at VP_05 for the charging slot(s). Charger capacity is 2 per slot. Are there interventions on charger capacity? None. So likely fine. But we must ensure that the charging does not conflict with other UAVs.\n\nSo the original baseline had MISSION_05 depart 2, MISSION_04 depart 6, no charge. But with initial SOC reduced, the baseline would actually fail the reserve check for MISSION_04, because after MISSION_05, SOC is 1.54633, then MISSION_04 consumes 0.09633, leaving 1.45, failing reserve. Wait, check baseline initial SOC for UAV_01: in the instance fleet data, UAV_01 initial_soc_kwh is 2.740825. But the intervention overrides it to 1.690825. So the baseline was designed with higher SOC. With the override, it becomes infeasible. So we need to adjust either departure slots or add charging.\n\nAlso, we have a pad capacity conflict at VP_05 slot 4. So we need to resolve both.\n\nNow look at UAV_03: MISSION_02 depart 2, PATH_06, COR_02, does it conflict with anything? COR_02 is not closed. Travel slots 2, arrival at VP_05 end of slot 3? Depart 2, travel 2 -> arrival end of slot 3, turnaround slot 4. Then MISSION_03 release 5, depart 5? But in baseline they depart 5, which is right after turnaround 4 (ready at start of 5). However, pad capacity at VP_05 slot 4: with capacity reduced to 1, if UAV_03 arrives at VP_05 end of slot 3, and does turnaround at slot 4, it will be on the ground at VP_05 during slot 4. At the same time, UAV_02 is idle at VP_05 since start, and UAV_01 if we adjust may also be there? That could exceed pad capacity 1 at slot 4. So we need to see all UAVs at VP_05 at slot 4.\n\nWe have three UAVs that might be at VP_05 at slot 4:\n- UAV_02: start at VP_05, available from slot 2, idle until depart 17. So UAV_02 occupies a pad slot at VP_05 continuously from slot 2 to 16 inclusive? Assuming idle occupies pad. So at slot 4, UAV_02 is there.\n- UAV_03 MISSION_02: if it departs at 2, travel 2, arrival end of slot 3, turnaround at slot 4 -> UAV_03 is on the pad at VP_05 during slot 4 (turnaround).\n- UAV_01 MISSION_05: in the original baseline, depart 2, travel 2, arrival end of slot 3, turnaround slot 4. So UAV_01 also on pad at slot 4. That makes 3 UAVs at VP_05 slot 4. With pad capacity override 1, that violates. So we need to adjust to avoid that. Options: delay one of the arrivals so they are not on the ground at slot 4, or hold a mission, or change paths? Since MISSION_05 destination is VP_05, it must land at VP_05. MISSION_02 destination is VP_05. So both will land at VP_05. The default pad capacity is 2, but override makes it 1 at slot 4. So we need to ensure that at most one UAV is on the ground (or arriving/departing?) during slot 4. If an aircraft departs at slot 4, that might also use pad. So we need to coordinate.\n\nWait, the pad capacity override applies only to slot 4? It might apply to slot 4 specifically. So only that one slot is restricted. So we could avoid conflicts by ensuring that at slot 4, no more than 1 UAV is present at VP_05. If we can stagger arrivals: MISSION_05 could be delayed to depart at slot 3, so it arrives end of slot 4? Depart 3, travel 2 -> flight slots 3,4, arrival end of slot 4, turnaround slot 5. Then during slot 4, UAV_01 is in the air, not on pad. So that removes UAV_01 from VP_05 ground at slot 4. MISSION_02: if it departs at 2, arrival end 3, turnaround 4, so UAV_03 on pad at slot 4. That's one. UAV_02 idle at VP_05 is also one. That gives two, still exceeding capacity 1. So we also need to avoid UAV_03 being on pad at slot 4. Can we delay MISSION_02? Release slot 2, due slot 4. Must depart no earlier than 2, and arrival must be by due 4? Due slot 4 means the mission must be completed by the end of slot 4? Usually due slot is the latest arrival slot. So if due slot is 4, the arrival must be at or before slot 4. If we depart at slot 3, travel 2, arrival end of slot 4? Depart 3, travel 2 -> flight 3,4, arrival end of 4, which is exactly slot 4 due. So that works. Then turnaround would be slot 5. That means UAV_03 is in the air during slot 4, not on pad. So that would remove UAV_03 from VP_05 at slot 4. So only UAV_02 idle on pad during slot 4. That meets capacity 1. So we can adjust both MISSION_05 and MISSION_02 departure slots to avoid the pad conflict.\n\nCheck MISSION_05: if delay to 3, due 8, fine. MISSION_02: delay to 3, due 4, arrival at end of 4 satisfies due? Typically \"due slot\" means the mission must be delivered by the end of that slot. So if depart at 3, travel 2, it arrives at the end of slot 4, so it meets due. Yes. Also check MISSION_02 energy: UAV_03 initial SOC 10.668856, PATH_06 energy 0.168856, reserve 2.5, so fine. No charge needed. Then MISSION_03: release 5, need to depart after MISSION_02. MISSION_02 turnaround slot 5, so UAV_03 becomes ready at start of slot 6. MISSION_03 release is 5, so it can depart at 6 or later. Due 22. But MISSION_03 path selection: PATH_05 is COR_08 closed, so must use alternative. We need to check PATH_07 or PATH_10. Also check energy: after MISSION_02, SOC = initial 10.668856 - 0.168856 = 10.5. Turnaround, then MISSION_03 service flight energy depends on path. PATH_07 energy 0.540338, PATH_10 energy 0.14496. Both then check reserve. After MISSION_03, need to do MISSION_06. So need to manage SOC throughout. Also MISSION_03 destination VP_06, from VP_05. So UAV_03 ends at VP_06. Then MISSION_06 origin VP_06, destination VP_01. Release 13, due 25. So we need to sequence properly.\n\nAlso, check if any corridor capacity conflicts. Corridors have capacity 2, but we need to check if multiple UAVs use the same corridor at the same time. The baseline might have such conflicts that need repair. But interventions don't change corridor capacities except closures. So we must ensure no more than 2 flights in a corridor per slot. We'll check that later.\n\nNow also need to check UAV_02: MISSION_01 depart 17, PATH_09 (COR_06). Start at VP_05. UAV_02 initial SOC 7.5, battery 16, reserve 1.5. PATH_09 energy 0.113, fine. No conflict. But wait, we moved MISSION_05 and MISSION_02 departure to 3, causing them to use corridors at slots 3-4. MISSION_05 uses COR_05 (from VP_04 to VP_05). MISSION_02 uses COR_02 (VP_04 to VP_05). Both are from VP_04 to VP_05? MISSION_05 origin VP_04, destination VP_05; MISSION_02 origin VP_04, destination VP_05. So both are flying from VP_04 to VP_05. They would do so around slots 3-4. Are there corridor capacity issues? MISSION_05 uses COR_05, MISSION_02 uses COR_02. Those are different corridors, so no conflict. So fine.\n\nNow check MISSION_03 with path choice. Since COR_08 is closed, we must use PATH_07 (COR_09) or PATH_10 (COR_11). We need to see which is feasible and optimal. Energy: PATH_07 uses 0.540338, PATH_10 uses 0.14496. Both are compatible (max wind level 1, UAV_03 max wind level 2). PATH_07 travel slots 2, PATH_10 travel slots 2. PATH_07 wind level 1, PATH_10 wind level 0. So PATH_07 is energy higher, PATH_10 lower. PATH_10 seems better in terms of energy and tardiness? But we must check due date and release. MISSION_03 due slot 22, release 5. With MISSION_02 departing 3 and turnaround 5, earliest MISSION_03 can depart is 6. So depart slot 6 is fine. If we use PATH_10, travel 2, arrival end of slot 7. Well before due 22. So no tardiness. PATH_07 also same. So we can choose PATH_10 to save energy. Also check if there is any closure on COR_11? No, COR_11 is not closed. So PATH_10 is valid. It uses corridor COR_11, wind level 0, compatible.\n\nNow MISSION_06: after MISSION_03, UAV_03 at VP_06. MISSION_06 origin VP_06, destination VP_01, path PATH_08 (COR_04). Release 13, due 25. With MISSION_03 depart 6, travel 2, arrival end of 7, turnaround 8, UAV_03 ready at start of 9. MISSION_06 release 13, so could depart at 13 earliest. That gives a gap of idle time from 9 to 12. That's fine. Depart 13, PATH_08 travel 1 slot, arrival end of 13, turnaround 14, done. Energy: after MISSION_03, SOC at arrival = SOC after MISSION_02 charge? Let's compute SOC after MISSION_02: 10.5, after turnaround, then MISSION_03 energy (PATH_10 0.14496), so SOC after MISSION_03 flight = 10.5 - 0.14496 = 10.35504. Reserve 2.5, fine. Then MISSION_06 energy PATH_08 0.570893, SOC after that = 9.784147, reserve check okay. So no charge needed. So UAV_03 can do with 0 charge blocks for all, but we might use charge after MISSION_03 if needed for later? No later mission for UAV_03. So no need.\n\nNow we need to revisit UAV_01. We had MISSION_05 depart 3 (to avoid pad), MISSION_04 release 6. But we found that without charging, SOC after MISSION_04 would be too low for reserve. So we must charge after MISSION_05. So add charge_blocks=1 after MISSION_05. That would increase SOC before MISSION_04. But we must check timing: MISSION_05 depart 3, travel 2, arrival end of 4, turnaround 5, then charge for 1 block (takes 1 slot? Each charge block takes 1 slot). So charging occurs after turnaround, i.e., during slot 6? The order: incoming deadhead (none) -> service flight (slots 3-4) -> reserve check (after arrival) -> destination turnaround (slot 5) -> post-service charge (slot 6 for charge_block=1). Then the UAV is ready for next mission at start of slot 7. But MISSION_04 release is 6, so earliest it could depart is 7? Actually after charging, the UAV can depart at the slot after the last charge block. So if charging occupies slot 6, it becomes available at start of slot 7. So MISSION_04 would need to depart at 7 or later. Due slot is 20, so fine. But release slot 6 means it cannot start before 6, but it can start at 7. So that works. But we must check if the departure at 7 is allowed given sequence order: MISSION_04 is seq 1 after MISSION_05. So we need to depart after MISSION_05 plus any turnaround/charge. Depart at 7 is after slot 6 charge, so ok.\n\nAlternative could be to use PATH_03 for MISSION_05? PATH_03 is another path from VP_04 to VP_05, using COR_10, distance 12 km, energy 1.356, travel slots 8, due slot 8 for MISSION_05. If we depart at 2, travel 8, arrival end of 9, which exceeds due 8, so tardy. So not good.\n\nSo UAV_01 schedule: MISSION_05 depart 3, PATH_01 (since PATH_03 is too long), charge_blocks=1 after. Then MISSION_04 depart 7, PATH_02, charge_blocks=0. Check energy: After MISSION_05, SOC = 1.690825 - 0.144495 = 1.54633. After turnaround and charge (1 block), SOC = 1.54633 + 1.2 = 2.74633. Then MISSION_04 energy 0.09633, remainder = 2.65, above reserve 1.5. So works.\n\nNow check pad/charger capacity at VP_05 for these slots. We have:\n- UAV_02 idle at VP_05 from slot 2 to 16. That occupies a pad continuously, reducing effective pad capacity. But pad capacity default is 2. With idle, it occupies 1. So if another UAV lands or departs at VP_05, we must ensure total on ground ≤ 2 (or ≤1 at slot 4). Override only at slot 4. So we must check other slots.\n\nAt slot 3: UAV_02 idle, UAV_01 and UAV_03 are flying (depart